    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for this. looks doable. i'm a bit worried about the deadlifts though, ive heard they're easy to mess up the form on. any specific tips for how to know if i'm doing it right or if i'm pushing too hard? also how should i handle the days when i feel really exhausted and just dont want to go to the gym?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Deadlifts require technique and form, so it's essential to start slowly to avoid injury. Here are some tips to ensure you're doing them correctly:\n\n- Keep your chest up and eyes forward, simulating trying to look somewhere in front of you.\n- Stay in a neutral spine, avoiding tilting or rounding your lower back.\n- Bend at the hips and knees, keeping your back straight throughout.\n- Hold the bar close to your body, gripping it just outside your legs.\n- Lift the bar from the floor with flat feet and pointed toes.\n\nIf you experience any pain or discomfort in these areas, it's advisable to consult a fitness professional before continuing.\n\nOn your more exhausted days, it's about striking a balance between exercise and recovery. Opt for:\n\n- Light stretching or yoga to maintain flexibility and promote circulation.\n- A walk at a modest pace for active recovery.\n- Listening to your body and understanding that rest days are just as important as workout days.\n\nRemember that rest is crucial for your body to recover and prevent overtraining, so don't be hard on yourself if the gym isn't appealing. You can come back with fresh energy.",
      "source": "target",
