    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the tips. one more thing—i've noticed that sometimes when i'm in the middle of a heavy set, i feel this sudden wave of mental fatigue or like my focus just vanishes, and i start feeling pretty unmotivated. is there a way to describe that feeling or manage it so it doesn't ruin my workout?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "What's known as \"pre-exhaustion,\" where your mental intensity fades just before a challenging set. If this happens, consider a few strategies:\n\n- Break the set into two: Complete one set to exhaustion, take a brief 30-second rest, and start the second set lighter if you can still perform well.\n- Use positive self-talk or a mantra to refocus before each set.\n- Shift your focus to the process, not just the result. Remind yourself why you started and celebrate the effort, not the weight lifted.\n\nThe goal isn't to continue lifting heavier but rather to find the balance between manageable and motivating. If mental fatigue repeatedly takes over, reassess your workout intensity and structure. It's still about progress, not perfection. Keep going, and remember that slight restorative work with compound lifts can still yield impressive long-term benefits.",
